  "id": "Compound::DB00761 - Gene::10723",
  "verbalization": "Potassium Chloride (KCl) is shown to interact with multiple members of the Slc12 family of proteins, specifically Slc12a6, Slc12a5, and Slc12a2, which are all involved in cation-coupled chloride cotransport. This suggests a potential role for KCl in modulating the activity of these transporters, which are critical for maintaining electrolyte balance and cellular homeostasis. The shared involvement of these transporters in the same functional pathway implies that KCl may influence their activity through a common mechanism, possibly by altering the electrochemical gradient or by affecting the conformational state of the proteins. The evidence from the three paths, with similar scores, supports a consistent interaction between KCl and the Slc12 family, suggesting a biologically plausible mechanism by which KCl could influence Slc12a7. The observed binding of KCl to these transporters and their participation in the same functional pathway provides a foundation for further investigation into the specific role of Slc12a7 in KCl-mediated transport and its potential implications in disease.",
